{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Oil and incense shall gladden the heart, and the sweetness of a friend, by counsel of the soul (Mishlei 27:9).</b>" "<b>Shlomo</b> the king, peace be upon him, admonishes a person in this verse (Mishlei 27) to have compassion on the stranger who has been exiled from his place and from the land of his birth. And because the Torah admonished concerning him in several places not to oppress him, neither with words nor with money — as it says (Exodus 22:20) ״וגר לא תונה״, and it is written (ibid. 23) ״וגר לא תלחץ״, and it is written (ibid.) ״ואתם ידעתם את נפש הגר״ — therefore Shlomo came and added and innovated here that for the man exiled and wandering from his place, a person is obligated in two things: the first, that he provide him his sustenance; the second, that he show him a cheerful countenance. And behold, this connects with the verse above that said (Mishlei 27:8): ״כצפור נודדת מן קנה כן איש נודד ממקומו״ — he compared the man who departed from the place where he was born to a bird leaving its nest, for there is the beginning of its existence and the cause of its generation. And immediately afterward he appended to it: ״שמן וקטרת״, calling all foods and everything cooked over the fire by the name 'oil,' and ״קטורת״ — that is the smoke rising from the food as it cooks. And he informed us through this that a person is obligated to gladden the heart of the man wandering from his place by providing sustenance, which is 'oil and incense,' as it says: ״שמן וקטרת ישמח לב״. And he is obligated further in showing a cheerful countenance, and this is ״ומתק רעהו״ — that he should sweeten his words to him and show him a cheerful face; for besides needing oil and incense, he further needs the sweetness of his friend, in sweetness of speech and gentleness of words. And he said ״מעצת נפש״ — that is, that sweetness and cheerful countenance should proceed from the counsel of the rational soul, in a manner of love and affection, and not in a manner of flattery. For sweetness of speech benefits him more than what he gives him. And so our Sages, of blessed memory, said in Ketubot: 'Better is one who whitens the teeth to his fellow than gives him milk to drink,' as it says (Genesis 49:12) ״ולבן שנים מחלב״ — do not read לבן שנים but rather לבון שנים. And so the prophet mentioned (Isaiah 58:10) ״הלא פרוס לרעב לחמך״ — and this is oil and incense — and he mentioned further (ibid.) ״ותפק לרעב נפשך״ — and this is the sweetness of his friend." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'Oil and incense shall gladden the heart' — this is the oil of the menorah, which was offered at the time of the incense, as it says (Exodus 30:8): ״ובהעלות אהרן את הנרות בין הערבים יקטירנה״ — it gladdens the heart of the Holy One, blessed be He.' They, of blessed memory, explained that just as Scripture found joy in the Holy One, blessed be He, after the work of Creation — which is what David said (Psalms 104:31): ״יהי כבוד ה׳ לעולם ישמח ה׳ במעשיו״ — so too we find joy in the Holy One, blessed be He, after the work of the Tabernacle, for He commanded the kindling of the menorah and the burning of the incense, and the Shekhinah dwelt in it. And concerning this Shlomo said: ״שמן וקטרת ישמח לב״. And it is already known that the work of the Tabernacle is equivalent to the work of Creation. And just as after the work of Creation we find that last of all there was created one who would recognize HaShem and worship Him and proclaim His unity — and that is man — so too after the work of the Tabernacle we find that it was necessary to command that there be in the Tabernacle one who worships and proclaims unity, and that is the High Priest. And since the Shekhinah dwelt in the Tabernacle by means of the incense, concerning this he said: ״שמן וקטרת ישמח לב״." "<b>And you already know</b> what is called קטורת — because the fragrance of the incense binds itself to the attributes, and the joy of the attributes increases upon the one who offers it, and through it blessing is drawn and poured forth to those below. And behold, this oil that was offered at the time of the burning of the incense was olive oil, because the olive brought light to the world, as it says (Genesis 8:11): ״והנה עלה זית טרף״, etc. Therefore the Torah commanded in the commandment of kindling the menorah with olive oil. This is what is written." "<b>And you shall command the children of Israel, and they shall take to you pure olive oil, crushed, for illumination, to kindle a lamp continually.</b> 'They shall take to you' — for that hour. For since it says at the end of the parashah ״חקת עולם״, I would have said that this commandment applies for all generations and not for that hour; therefore it was necessary to say 'to you' — for that hour — to tell you that the commandment applies for that hour and for all generations. And it mentions 'olive oil' to exclude all other oils: sesame oil, walnut oil, and similar kinds. And it says ״זית זך״ — that is, the first oil, to exclude the second and the third. ״כתית״ — oil that comes by way of crushing, to exclude oil that flows out on its own. ״למאור״ — crushed for illumination, and not for meal-offerings: the first for the menorah, and the second for meal-offerings. And the way of the world is that one who has inferior oil burns it in a lamp, and one who has good oil puts it into food and eats it — but here it is the reverse: the first, which is the pure, is for illumination, and the second for meal-offerings." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> כתית — this is the First Temple, which stood 410 years; the Second Temple, 420 years. And this Midrash alludes to the two Temples, and it would have been fitting to allude also to the third, future one." "<b>But</b> it is possible to say that this Midrash may perhaps allude to those Temples whose time was fixed; and since both of them were destined to be destroyed and to become trampled, therefore the allusion concerning both of them was expressed in the language of כתית, which is the language of crushing and trampling. But the third, future House — although it was not included in the reckoning because its time is not fixed — behold, it is alluded to in the word למאור, in the manner of (Isaiah 60:1): ״קומי אורי כי בא אורך״ — and this is a promise concerning the third Temple, at the return of the Shekhinah; and this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 27:1): ״ה' אורי וישעי״, and he further said (Psalms 118:27): ״אל ה' ויאר לנו״. And since there is no limit to the duration of its standing, but it stands forever and eternally, therefore it says להעלות נר תמיד — that is, that its exaltation is constant and never again ceases." "<b>To cause a lamp to ascend.</b> The singular is a collective noun, like נרות; and so (Genesis 32:6): ״ויהי לי שור וחמור״, and so (Judges 21:16): ״כי נשמדה מבנימן אשה״, and so (Ezekiel 1:22): ״ודמות על ראשי החיה״ — and there were four חיות." "<b>Continually.</b> That the Menorah should not remain without a lamp; or one may say: a lamp that is perpetual, alluding to the western lamp, which is the principal of all the lamps, for all the lamps faced toward it, and it faced toward the Shekhinah, which is the tenth sefirah; and this is the meaning of תמיד. And understand this." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> להעלות נר תמיד — this is what the verse says (Job 14:15): ״תקרא ואנכי אענך למעשה ידיך תכסוף״ — You bear the entire world, as it is said (Isaiah 46:4): ״אני עשיתי ואני אשא ואני אסבול ואמלט״, and You command the children of Kehat to bear the Ark of Your glory on the shoulder, as it is said (Numbers 7:9): ״ולבני קהת לא נתן כי עבודת הקדש עליהם בכתף ישאו״. You sustain the entire world, and You command (Numbers 28:2): ״את קרבני לחמי״. You illuminate the entire world, and You command להעלות נר תמיד — hence (Job 14:15): ״למעשה ידיך תכסוף״. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said: Not that I require the light of flesh and blood, for through My light I illuminate the entire world, but rather in order to exalt you and confer merit upon you. And further there, Rabbi Meir said: The Holy One, blessed be He, said: The lamps that Aaron kindles are beloved to Me more than the luminaries that I set in the heavens — because at the time when the princes offered the dedication of the altar and the tribe of Levi did not offer, the soul of Aaron was grieved. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: All the tribes offered one dedication, and you alone shall make a dedication of your own; as it is written (Psalms 10:17): ״תאות ענוים שמעת ה'״. And this is what is written: מחוץ לפרוכת וגו׳ יערוך אותו אהרן ובניו — end of quotation. And it may be an allusion to the dedication of the lamps of the Hasmoneans; and if so, when it says יערוך אותו אהרן ובניו, it alludes both to that hour and to the generations."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:05:29.396796103Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}